  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nScattered rain showers will continue this morning and gradually \nshift northeast of the area by mid to late afternoon, as upper-\nlevel wave moves across Kansas today and shifts into Missouri \ntonight. Cloudy skies and light southeast winds with some patchy\nareas of drizzle will be possible this Halloween evening. \nThings dry out for Sunday as upper level ridging builds over \nKansas. Meanwhile current satellite water vapor imagery shows \nnext weather system over the eastern Pacific. This system will \nmove east across the Rockies and eject out into the central \nplains on Monday causing south winds to increase. The \natmospheric destabilization potential hinges primarily on the \ntiming of this upper-level wave ejecting into Kansas. Should \nmorning showers/storms develop and persist into the afternoon, \ndestabilization could be limited. However, wind shear levels are\nforecasted to strengthen throughout the afternoon and night \ntime hours. Consequently, if instability builds alongside the \nincreasing wind shear, a few strong to severe storms may develop\non Monday.\n\nModels show upper level system gradually shifting east of Kansas for \nlate Tuesday afternoon with deformation zone of lift and associated \nrain showers pivoting across the area during the day. A lull in the \nprecipitation activity looks to follow for Wednesday and Thursday \nwith daytime highs near seasonal values for early November.",
